@CathieDWood believes rates could be rising for a different reason than inflation. Zoom out to 230 years of history and today's long-term rates sit near the low end of the 3% to 6% range that held for 130 years before the Great Depression. She thinks accelerating real growth, not inflation, may be driving this move.

I (well, my agent) logged every tokenomics change across the 309 tokens that have sat in the Top 100 since 2021. Each one sourced to a governance vote or an announcement. Burns, buybacks, fee switches, emission cuts.

Net of the changes that went against holders, they've gone from 10 a year in 2021-22 to 32 a year now. More than 3x.

The trend is clear. Product-market fit remains paramount. But on its own, it won't be enough. Protocols need to show supply discipline and value accrual alongside it.

Short vibes. Long fundamentals.

